/**

 @page benchmarks_multigrid_petsc_poisson Poisson equation

 We solve the Poisson equation 
 
 @f$ -\Delta u = 2 \pi^2 \Pi _d \sin (\pi x_d) @f$ 
 
 over the unit square or cube, respectively.


 # Prepare the executable
 
 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 export PYTHONPATH=../../../../python/
 python3 fem.py -m release
 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 
 # Visualise the outcome
 
 To visualise the outcome, most people translate the resulting Peano patch file
 into paraview with a command similar to
 
 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 /opt/paraview/bin/pvpython ~/git/Peano/python/peano4/visualisation/render.py --filter-fine-grid solution-CollocatedPoisson.peano-patch-file
 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

 # Solver description
 
 ## Discontinuous Galerkin
 
 A DG solver is instantiated similar to
 

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ 
solver = multigrid.petsc.solvers.DiscontinuousGalerkinDiscretisation(name                 = "DGPoisson",
                                                                     polynomial_degree    = args.degree,
                                                                     cell_unknowns        = 1,                      # unknowns_per_cell_dof,
                                                                     face_unknowns        = 1,                      # unknowns_per_cell_dof,
                                                                     dimensions           = args.dimensions,
                                                                     min_h                = args.meshsize,
                                                                     max_h                = args.meshsize,
                                                                     cell_cell_rhs_matrix   = matrices.getCellCellMassMatrix(),     
                                                                     cell_cell_lhs_matrix   = matrices.getCellCellSystemMatrixForLaplacian(),
                                                                     cell_cell_lhs_matrix_scaling = args.dimensions-2,
                                                                     cell_cell_rhs_matrix_scaling = args.dimensions,
                                                                     cell_to_face_matrix     = matrices.getCellToFaceMatrix(),       
                                                                     face_to_cell_matrix     = matrices.getCellIdentityMatrix(), 
                                                                     cell_to_face_matrix_scaling  = 0,       
                                                                     face_to_cell_matrix_scaling  = args.dimensions, 
                                                                     face_face_Riemann_problem_matrix        = matrices.getAveragingRiemannSolver(),   # todo bad name    
#                                                                     quadrature_points_in_unit_interval = matrices.getPointsInUnitInterval(),
                                                                     quadrature_points_in_unit_interval = [0.0, 1.0],
                                                                     )
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ 

 We follow the notation as used for multigrid.petsc.solvers.DiscontinuousGalerkinDiscretisation
 and obtain, for Poisson, @f$ \mathcal{L}(u) = -\Delta u = - \nabla ^2 u = - \partial_x^2 u - \partial_y^2 u @f$.
 The extension to higher dimensions is straightforward.
 Our weak formulation becomes


\f{eqnarray*}{
  \int _\Omega \mathcal{L}(u) \cdot v \ dx 
  &=& 
  \int _\Omega ( -\Delta u) \cdot v \ dx
  \\ 
  &=& 
  \int _{\text{cell}_v} (- \Delta u) \cdot v \ dx
  \\ 
  &=& 
  \int _{\text{cell}_v} ( \nabla u, \nabla v) \ dx
  - 
  \int _{\partial \text{cell}_v} (\hat{\nabla u},n) \cdot v \ dx
  =
  \int _{\text{cell}_v} f \cdot v dx 
\f}


  Within the framework above, we end up with the following situation:

  - Each cell holds @f$ (p+1)^d @f$ degrees of freedom (dof). We specify this 
    through the variable polynomial_degree. 
  - Each node within the cell holds one unknown as we solve a scalar equation.
    Therefore, 
    
            cell_unknowns=1
            
  - The operator @f$ A_{cc} @f$ takes the dofs within a cell and couples
    them with each other. It respresents @f$ \int _{\text{cell}_v} ( \nabla u, \nabla v) \ dx @f$.
  - On the faces, we have the gradient @f$ \nabla u @f$ from the left and from
    right right. So we might be tempted to hold @f$ \nabla u^+ @f$ and
    @f$ \nabla u^- @f$. So @f$ 2d @f$ quantities in total. However, this is 
    not necessary: We are really only interested in the gradient along the 
    normal. Therefore, we set 
     
            face_unknowns=1 
    
    These are not really unknowns in the linear algebra sense. They are 
    helpers.        
  - The operator @f$ P_{cf} @f$ takes the solution from a cell and projects it 
    the faces. Hence, @f$ P_{cf} \in \mathbb{R}^{2d(p+1)^{d-1} \times (p+1)^{d}} @f$.
    The 2d equals the number of faces of a cell, while the cell is a submanifold
    and therefore hosts @f$ (p+1)^{d-1} @f$. 
    The result is written into 
    @f$ u^+ @f$ or @f$ u^- @f$ respectively on the faces.
  - The operator @f$ A_{ff} @f$ takes the @f$ 2(p+1)^{d-1} @f$ on the face
    and yields the 
    @f$ (p+1)^{d-1} @f$ averages.
  - The operator @f$ P_{fc} @f$ takes the @f$ 2d(p+1)^{d-1} @f$ values @f$ \hat u @f$ on the face and yields
    the updates for the cell. 
  
 In the example above, we use d-linear shape functions arranged along the 
 Gauss-Lobatto integration points. So the two nodes carrying the shape 
 function weights are spread out over the unit interval.
 
 
 */
 